from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('12');add=A.add
add('3.1-01','The kinetic particle model describes matter using moving particles, spaces and interactions.','Heating within a phase generally increases their mean kinetic energy rather than making individual particles larger.','The model connects invisible microscopic behaviour with observable shape, volume, pressure and temperature. Particles in a solid vibrate about positions; those in liquids move among close neighbours; gas particles move widely through the available volume. A drawing is a model, so particle sizes and gaps are not necessarily to scale. Use the different parts together rather than using motion alone to explain every property.','Explain how the particle model links a gas’s temperature and pressure at fixed volume.','Combine the links: higher temperature means greater mean particle kinetic energy.~Faster particles transfer momentum to the walls more strongly and frequently, increasing pressure.')
add('3.1-01-M01','Matter consists of particles separated by spaces.','Gases have much larger typical separations than solids and liquids.','The spaces are not filled by smaller pieces of the same gas in this model. When compressing a gas, the sample volume mainly decreases by reducing separation; individual particles are not squeezed appreciably smaller. Close particle spacing in liquids and solids helps explain their much smaller compressibility.','Explain why a gas sample can be compressed substantially.','Its particles are widely separated.~Pressure can reduce the spaces between them without greatly changing individual particle size.')
add('3.1-01-M02','Interactions between particles help hold condensed matter together.','Particle separation and motion affect how strongly these interactions influence the sample.','In a solid, particles remain near stable positions while vibrating. Liquid particles remain close but can change neighbours. Widely separated gas particles spend much of their travel with little intermolecular influence in the simple model. Heating does not switch off every interaction; it changes motion and can alter arrangement.','Explain why a solid holds its shape in the particle model.','Interactions keep its particles near fixed relative positions.~They vibrate there rather than freely exchanging positions throughout the sample.')
add('3.1-01-M03','Particles are in continual motion at ordinary temperatures.','Solid particles vibrate; liquid and gas particles can move from place to place.','A stationary container does not imply stationary molecules inside it. Their random motions can cancel in an overall bulk velocity while individual particles continue moving. Heating generally increases the average kinetic energy rather than directing every molecule the same way.','Explain why a stationary gas sample still contains moving particles.','Particle motions are random in direction.~Their bulk effects can balance even though individual particles continue travelling.')
add('3.1-01-M04','The simple gas model treats particle encounters as conserving total kinetic energy during the encounter.','Individual particles can exchange energy and change direction.','This microscopic model helps describe persistent random motion without requiring particles to slow after each mutual encounter. The energy of one particle need not stay fixed because it can transfer energy to another. The conservation statement concerns their combined kinetic energy in the model, not identical speeds before and after.','Explain why two interacting gas particles can leave with different speeds while the simple encounter model conserves kinetic energy.','Energy can transfer between the two particles.~The combined kinetic energy is conserved, although each particle’s share can change.')
add('3.1-01-M05','Temperature is related to the mean kinetic energy of particles.','A warmer sample has a larger average kinetic energy in the particle model.','Particles do not all have the same speed, and a temperature increase does not require every particle to have more energy at every instant. Distinguish the mean from one selected particle. A phase change can absorb energy without raising temperature because the energy affects interactions and arrangement rather than the mean kinetic energy.','Explain what a temperature rise indicates about a gas’s particles.','Their mean kinetic energy has increased.~Their random motion is more energetic on average.')
add('3.1-02','Expansion, compression and diffusion describe different particle behaviours.','Expansion commonly increases separation, compression reduces it and diffusion redistributes particles by random motion.','A warmed metal rod expands because mean spacing changes, not because new atoms appear. A gas compresses readily because of its large gaps. A dye spreads in still liquid because its particles and the liquid molecules move randomly. Avoid replacing diffusion with a bulk current: convection can mix substances too, but it is a different mechanism.','Explain why gas compression and diffusion both depend on particle behaviour but describe different changes.','Combine the ideas: compression reduces average separation and sample volume.~Diffusion redistributes particles through random motion without requiring overall compression.')
add('3.1-02-M01','Most solids expand when heated over an ordinary temperature range.','Increased vibration is associated with a larger mean separation of neighbouring particles.','A ball may fit through a ring when cool but fail after heating the ball. Its atoms are not individually enlarged in the model; their mean arrangement changes. Different materials expand by different amounts, so joints may need room for movement. The claim is limited because some materials have unusual behaviour over particular ranges.','Explain ordinary thermal expansion of a metal rod in particle terms.','Heating increases particle vibration.~Their mean separation increases, lengthening the rod.')
add('3.1-02-M02','Liquids and gases commonly expand when heated if the container permits expansion.','The volume change is associated with altered average separation, rather than an increase in particle number.','A liquid column rises in a narrow tube because a small volume increase produces a visible length change. For a gas held at fixed volume, heating instead increases pressure. State the container constraint before predicting an observed change. Water near 4 °C is a known exception to a simple general expansion rule.','Explain why a heated liquid can rise in a narrow tube.','Heating increases the liquid’s volume over the stated ordinary range.~The extra volume occupies a greater length of the narrow tube.')
add('3.1-02-M03','Gases are much more compressible than ordinary liquids or solids.','The main difference is the much greater space between gas particles.','Closing a trapped-air syringe can reduce its sample volume markedly. A sealed water-filled syringe moves much less under a comparable pressure change because its particles are already close. Do not infer that a gas has no forces or that a liquid is mathematically incompressible; the comparison is qualitative.','Explain why trapped air compresses more readily than water.','Air has much greater spaces between particles.~Those spaces can decrease, whereas water particles already lie close together.')
add('3.1-02-M04','Diffusion is spreading caused by random particle motion.','The net redistribution tends to reduce concentration differences without requiring a directed bulk flow.','A smell can spread through air and dye can spread through water even without deliberate stirring. Individual particles move in varied directions, but initially more leave a concentrated region than enter it. Warmer conditions often speed this process because particle motion is more energetic. A draught or stirred current may also transport material and should be distinguished in an experiment.','Explain why dye spreads through still water.','Dye and water particles move randomly.~This motion redistributes dye from a concentrated region through more of the water.')
add('3.1-03','Thermal expansion can be useful or can produce damage if constrained.','Different expansion rates can bend a bonded bimetallic strip.','A bridge joint permits length changes rather than forcing a heated structure against a fixed boundary. A bimetallic strip uses different expansion amounts to bend and can operate a switch. Both involve expansion, but one design accommodates it while the other uses differential expansion as an action. Do not claim expansion gaps themselves generate motion or electricity.','Explain the difference between an expansion joint and a bimetallic strip as uses of expansion.','A joint provides space for a structure’s changing length.~A bimetallic strip uses unequal expansion of bonded metals to produce bending.')
add('3.1-04','Phase properties follow from particle arrangement, mobility and interaction.','A solid keeps shape, a liquid flows at nearly fixed volume and a gas fills its container.','A liquid takes the shape of its container’s lower region because particles can exchange neighbours while remaining close. A gas has no comparable fixed sample volume at ordinary pressure, because particles spread through the available space. Solid particles remain mobile through vibration, so fixed shape must not be explained by complete lack of particle motion.','Explain how liquid and gas particle models account for different sample volumes.','Link close liquid spacing to its nearly fixed volume.~Widely separated gas particles spread throughout the available container volume.')
add('3.1-04-M01','Solid and liquid particles are close together; gas particles are widely separated.','Solids commonly have more ordered relative positions, while liquid positions can rearrange.','Show equal particle sizes in a simple diagram when comparing the same substance across phases. Change their spacing and arrangement, not the number of particles to represent a fixed sample. A solid drawing should show vibrations about positions; liquid arrows allow rearrangement; gas arrows show random travel in the available space.','Explain the particle-arrangement difference between a liquid and a gas of the same substance.','Liquid particles are close together although irregularly arranged.~Gas particles are much farther apart and occupy the available volume.')
add('3.1-04-M02','Solid particles mainly vibrate about positions, while liquid particles can move past neighbours.','Gas particles travel freely between encounters in the simple model.','A flowing liquid is not evidence that it has no attractions; its cohesion and near-fixed volume show interactions remain important. A solid’s fixed shape likewise does not mean its particles are stationary. Compare motion and forces together to avoid those incomplete explanations.','Explain why a liquid flows while a solid normally keeps its shape.','Liquid particles can move past one another, whereas solid particles remain near fixed relative positions.~Solid interactions maintain the arrangement while permitting vibration.')
add('3.1-05','During a pure substance’s phase change at fixed pressure, temperature can remain constant while energy transfers.','The energy changes particle interactions and arrangement rather than mean kinetic energy.','A melting solid absorbs energy to loosen its ordered structure. Freezing releases energy as a more ordered condensed arrangement forms. On a heating curve, a plateau therefore does not mean no energy enters. Impurities, changing pressure and mixed observations can complicate the ideal plateau, so state the intended pure-substance model.','Explain why ice can absorb energy during melting without a temperature rise at fixed pressure.','The absorbed energy changes the particle arrangement and interactions.~Mean kinetic energy does not rise during the ideal phase change, so temperature remains constant.')
add('3.1-06','Melting, freezing, boiling, condensation and sublimation are phase changes.','Energy is absorbed when separation or freedom increases and released in the reverse changes under corresponding conditions.','Melting changes solid to liquid; freezing reverses it. Vaporisation changes liquid to gas and condensation reverses it. Sublimation goes directly from solid to gas, and deposition is the reverse. Water on the outside of a cold cup comes from condensation of atmospheric water vapour, rather than liquid leaking through an intact cup.','Explain why liquid droplets can appear outside a cold glass.','Nearby water vapour transfers energy to the cooler surface.~It condenses into liquid droplets when conditions permit.')
add('3.1-07','A gas’s temperature describes its molecular motion through mean kinetic energy.','Bulk movement and random thermal movement are different.','A gas can have high temperature while its container is stationary. Blowing air in one direction gives a bulk velocity, but temperature concerns random motion rather than that overall flow alone. Compare samples using their mean kinetic energy, not simply the speed of one molecule selected at an instant.','Explain why stationary hot air can have a higher temperature than a cooler moving breeze.','Temperature concerns mean random molecular kinetic energy.~Bulk flow speed alone does not determine that thermal energy measure.')
add('3.1-08','Gas pressure arises from particles transferring momentum to container walls.','The many wall impacts produce an average force per area.','Each impact changes a particle’s momentum and exerts a force on the wall. More frequent or stronger momentum transfers per area increase pressure. Pressure is not the gas’s weight pressing solely on the bottom; a gas also acts on side walls and the lid. The random directions account for pressure throughout the enclosure.','Explain how gas molecules exert pressure on a container wall.','Their impacts change molecular momentum and exert forces on the wall.~The combined average force per unit area is the gas pressure.')
add('3.1-09','Heating a fixed amount of gas at constant volume increases its pressure in the simple model.','The higher mean kinetic energy increases the rate of momentum transfer to the walls.','The amount of gas must remain fixed and the vessel must not expand substantially for this comparison. Faster particles reach walls more often and have larger typical momentum changes. The syllabus treatment here is qualitative; do not use a Celsius-temperature ratio as a proportional gas law.','Explain why warming a sealed rigid gas container raises its pressure.','Particles gain mean kinetic energy and move faster.~At the fixed volume they transfer momentum to the walls at a greater rate.')
add('3.1-10','Heating a fixed amount of gas at constant pressure increases its volume in the simple model.','Expansion offsets the increased molecular motion so pressure can remain unchanged.','A movable piston under a fixed load can rise as the gas warms. The larger volume reduces the rate of wall impacts compared with the heated gas held in its original volume. State constant pressure rather than constant volume for this prediction. Only qualitative behaviour is required here.','Explain why a gas under a freely moving piston can expand when warmed at constant external pressure.','Warmer particles tend to increase wall momentum transfer.~The piston moves outward and the larger volume allows the gas pressure to balance the unchanged external pressure.')
add('3.1-11','Reducing a fixed gas sample’s volume at constant temperature increases its pressure.','The same mean kinetic energy is associated with more frequent impacts per wall area in the smaller space.','Compress slowly enough for heat exchange to maintain temperature in the intended comparison. Rapid compression can raise temperature and adds another effect. A pressure increase from bringing the particles closer does not require each particle to grow or acquire greater mean kinetic energy in this isothermal model.','Explain why slowly compressing a fixed gas sample at constant temperature raises its pressure.','Particles encounter walls more frequently in the smaller volume.~Momentum transfer per wall area and time increases despite unchanged mean kinetic energy.')
add('3.1-12','Brownian motion is irregular random movement of small particles suspended in a fluid.','It provides indirect evidence of molecular motion.','Observed smoke or pollen particles are much larger than the surrounding molecules. Their erratic zigzag motion differs from a common drift of the whole fluid. Use a microscope and suitable illumination to observe the suspended particles, recognising that individual fluid molecules are not what the ordinary school view shows.','State the motion observed in small suspended particles that supports the kinetic particle model.','Irregular random zigzag motion, called Brownian motion.')
add('3.1-13','Suspended particles undergo uneven bombardment by randomly moving fluid molecules.','Changing imbalance of these impacts produces their erratic motion.','A particle can receive a temporarily larger push from one direction, then from another. The continually changing imbalance prevents a smooth directed path. A larger suspended body averages many impacts more evenly, making the visible jitter less pronounced. The suspended particle is evidence for molecular motion rather than itself being one gas molecule.','Explain Brownian motion in terms of surrounding molecules.','Fluid molecules strike the suspended particle randomly and unevenly.~The changing resultant of those impacts gives its irregular motion.')
C=A.choice
C('3.1-01-M01','Which phase is usually most compressible?',('Gas','Its particles have large separations.'),('Solid','Particles are already close.'),('Liquid','It has relatively little free space.'),('All three equally','Their typical separations differ.'))
C('3.1-01-M03','How do solid particles move in the model?',('Vibrate about positions','This permits motion while preserving shape.'),('Remain completely still','Ordinary thermal motion persists.'),('Travel freely throughout a room','That is a gas-like description.'),('Disappear and reappear','That is not the model.'))
C('3.1-01-M05','What increases when a gas’s temperature rises?',('Mean molecular kinetic energy','Temperature tracks this average.'),('Size of each molecule','Expansion is not molecular enlargement.'),('Number of molecules by itself','Heating need not add material.'),('Its mass necessarily doubles','No such mass change follows.'))
C('3.1-02-M04','What causes diffusion in a still fluid?',('Random particle motion','It redistributes particles.'),('A required bulk current','Diffusion can occur without that flow.'),('Particles becoming larger','That does not explain spreading.'),('Gravity acting in one horizontal direction','That is not the random-motion mechanism.'))
C('3.1-05','Why can temperature remain fixed during pure melting?',('Energy changes arrangement and interactions','The mean kinetic energy need not rise.'),('No energy is supplied','Melting can absorb energy.'),('All particles stop','Particles remain in motion.'),('The material has no mass','The sample remains present.'))
C('3.1-12','What is directly seen in a Brownian-motion demonstration?',('Small suspended particles','The fluid molecules bombard them.'),('Individual gas molecules with an ordinary microscope','They are not resolved by that observation.'),('The absence of collisions','Uneven bombardment causes the motion.'),('Motion along one smooth fixed path','The observed path is irregular.'))
C('3.1-08','What produces gas pressure at a wall?',('Molecular momentum transfer','Impacts produce average force per area.'),('Only gas weight at the floor','Pressure also acts sideways and upward.'),('No molecular movement','The model requires motion.'),('The colour of the container','That is not the pressure mechanism.'))
C('3.1-09','A fixed gas amount is heated in a rigid sealed vessel. What happens?',('Pressure rises','Mean kinetic energy rises at fixed volume.'),('Pressure falls because particles slow','Heating increases mean energy.'),('Volume expands freely','The vessel is rigid.'),('Molecules are added through the seal','The amount is fixed.'))
C('3.1-10','A gas is warmed at constant pressure under a movable piston. What happens?',('Volume rises','The piston can move outward.'),('Volume must remain fixed','That would impose a different constraint.'),('Mean kinetic energy falls','Warming increases it.'),('The gas becomes a solid immediately','That is not the stated change.'))
C('3.1-11','A fixed gas amount is compressed isothermally. What happens?',('Pressure rises','Wall impacts become more frequent.'),('Mean kinetic energy rises by definition','Temperature is held constant.'),('Particles become larger','Separation changes instead.'),('Pressure falls','That reverses the qualitative relation.'))
C('3.1-03','Why leave a gap between sections of a metal structure?',('To accommodate thermal length changes','This reduces stress when it expands.'),('To prevent any particle motion','Thermal vibration remains.'),('To make atoms expand individually','That is not the spacing model.'),('To create new metal','No new matter is required.'))
C('3.1-06','What forms on the outside of a sufficiently cold intact glass in humid air?',('Condensed water from the air','Vapour loses energy near the cold surface.'),('Water passing through the solid glass','An intact wall need not leak.'),('A new gas from frozen glass','That is not the phase change.'),('Water created from nothing','Matter is redistributed, not created.'))
P=A.part
P('3.1-12','State the name for irregular motion of suspended particles.','Brownian motion.',1,'One mark for Brownian motion.')
P('3.1-04-M01','State which phase has widely separated particles.','Gas.',1,'One mark for gas.')
P('3.1-08','Explain molecular pressure on a wall.','Impacts transfer momentum to the wall and exert force. The average force per area is pressure.',2,'One mark for impact/momentum transfer; one for force per area.')
P('3.1-09','Explain the pressure change when a fixed gas amount is heated at fixed volume.','Mean molecular kinetic energy rises. Greater momentum transfer to the walls per time raises pressure.',2,'One mark for increased molecular energy; one for the wall-transfer consequence.')
P('3.1-11','Explain the pressure change when that sample is compressed while temperature stays fixed.','Wall impacts become more frequent in the smaller volume. Pressure rises while mean molecular kinetic energy remains unchanged.',2,'One mark for impact frequency; one for rising pressure under the temperature condition.')
A.deviations.append(dict(from_='Two numerical applications in the structured-task template',to='Two particle-model applications at two marks each.',why='The syllabus specifies qualitative gas-law treatment; numerical gas-law equations would exceed the intended depth.'))
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot column length vertically against temperature horizontally for the stated limited range; fit the approximately straight trend.',unit='mm/°C',meaning='The gradient is change in liquid-column length per temperature degree for this apparatus. It is not the intrinsic volume expansion coefficient without further geometry.',method=['Use a safe liquid-filled bulb connected to an open narrow uniform tube.','Place the bulb in a stirred water bath with a separate calibrated thermometer.','Wait for equilibrium at a chosen moderate temperature and record the liquid-column length.','Change bath temperature in stages and repeat once both readings settle.','Repeat on cooling, using the same bulb, tube and liquid amount.'],control='Keep tube geometry and liquid amount fixed, remove bubbles, avoid sealing a heated apparatus and handle warm water carefully.',table='Record bath temperature / °C and liquid-column length / mm, with repeated readings.',conclusion='A rising approximately linear length-temperature trend supports thermal expansion over the tested range; the slope describes this instrument’s response.')
if __name__=='__main__':A.build()
